  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 337 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nA deep upper trough parked over sourthern Canada near the Pacific \nNorthwest will move eastward through Tuesday into Wednesday and \nflatten out the upper ridge that is over much of the Plains. During \nthis timeframe near the surface, a cold front will push across the \nnorthern Plains throughout late Monday through Tuesday. This front \nwill come into contact with a moist airmass provided by strong \nsouthwest flow ahead of it. Combine this with modest but sufficient \nwind shear, increased chances for isolated showers and thunderstorms \nwill linger into Tuesday morning. Once the front passes through, \nslightly cooler temperatures will be in its wake for the remainder \nof the week, with highs in the upper 80s to to potentially low 90s \nfurther south along I80. Wednesday in particular will be the coolest \nwith highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s across the region. \n\nThe flattened ridge will become a more zonal flow regime through the \nmid week with high pressure settling across Nebraska. This is likely \nto preclude precipitation chances until the following weekend where \na deepening upper trough is forecasted to take shape across the \nPacific Northwest sometime Saturday morning. Once this trough \ncrosses the Rockies, it will support increasing precipitation \nchances as well as the potential fore returning to near normal highs \nfor the time of year in the low to mid 80s.",
